uestion/math-academy-id "q-118593" :question/problem "The mass $M(t),$ in micrograms, of a sample of rhodium-101 is given by\n\n$$\nM(t)=7{e}^{-0.21t},\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ is the time, in years. Rounded to one decimal place, what is the half-life of rhodium-101?" :question/worked-solution "The mass $M(t)$ of a radioactive substance at time $t$ can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n{M}^{′}(t)=-kM(t),\\,M(0)={M}_{0}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nM(t)={M}_{0}{e}^{-kt}.\n$$\n\nThe half-life ${t}_{1/2}$ is the time taken for the mass of the substance to reduce by one half and is given by\n\n$$\n{t}_{1/2}=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{k}.\n$$\n\nWe're given that $k=0.21.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}_{1/2} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{k} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{0.21} \\\\  & ≈3.3\\,\\text{years}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-118593/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "589c680e-9f93-58e9-9dab-c34de98d7225"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-118593"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-353546/field-1/f0eb0953a0cd83f9e8ba235bc4297e3fc0a57f8136369390e4388df6dde8335c" :answer/id #uuid "be4ffe4f-7ae7-5f86-8417-329f40a99bc2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "415.2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-353546/field-1/13530cb3443be755565f26253881484f0fbb9a4588835b964c3963a7a91f082f" :answer/id #uuid "17ca6d1f-d70a-55e9-a3c5-ef3c77fec547" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "276.8" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-353546/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "97a41f62-c155-5397-8795-7d9044a01696" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-353546/field-1/f0eb0953a0cd83f9e8ba235bc4297e3fc0a57f8136369390e4388df6dde8335c" :answer-field/choices ["q-353546/field-1/f0eb0953a0cd83f9e8ba235bc4297e3fc0a57f8136369390e4388df6dde8335c" "q-353546/field-1/13530cb3443be755565f26253881484f0fbb9a4588835b964c3963a7a91f082f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-353546" :question/id #uuid "7f844e0a-d105-5cbd-ae82-24250701f497" :question/math-academy-id "q-353546" :question/problem "The half-life of polonium-210 is $138.4$ days. How long does it take for a $8.8\\,\\mu \\text{g}$ sample of polonium-210 to reduce to $1.1\\,\\mu \\text{g}?$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$t=$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\text{days}$" :question/worked-solution "The mass $M(t)$ of a radioactive substance at time $t$ can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n{M}^{′}(t)=-kM(t),\\,M(0)={M}_{0}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nM(t)={M}_{0}{e}^{-kt}.\n$$\n\nThe half-life ${t}_{1/2}$ is the time taken for the mass of the substance to reduce by one half and is given by\n\n$$\n{t}_{1/2}=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{k}.\n$$\n\nThere are two methods that we can use to solve this problem. We describe each of them below.\n\n**Method 1**\n\nSince we know that ${t}_{1/2}=138.4$ days, we have\n\n$$\n138.4=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{k}\\,⇒\\,k=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{138.4}\n$$\n\nAlso, since ${M}_{0}=8.8\\,\\mu \\text{g},$ we have the particular solution\n\n$$\nM(t)=8.8{e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/138.4}.\n$$\n\nWe wish to calculate the time $t$ such that $M(t)=1.1\\,\\mu \\text{g}.$ So, we set up our equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1.1 & =8.8{e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/138.4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1.1 & =8.8{e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/138.4} \\\\ \\frac{1.1}{8.8} & ={e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/138.4} \\\\ \\frac{1}{8} & ={e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/138.4} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{8}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/138.4}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡8 & =-\\frac{t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{138.4} \\\\ -\\operatorname{ln}⁡8 & =-\\frac{t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{138.4} \\\\ t & =\\frac{138.4\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡8}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} \\\\ t & =\\frac{138.4\\cdot 3\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} \\\\ t & =415.2\\,\\text{days}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nWe wish to calculate the time $t$ such that $M(t)=1.1\\,\\mu \\text{g}.$ Notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{M(t)}{M(0)} & =\\frac{1.1\\,\\mu \\text{g}}{8.8\\,\\mu \\text{g}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8} \\\\  & ={(\\frac{1}{2})}^{3},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nor equivalently\n\n$$\nM(t)={(\\frac{1}{2})}^{3}\\cdot M(0).\n$$\n\nFrom this equation, we see that $3$ half-lives must have occurred for the mass to reduce from $8.8\\,\\mu \\text{g}$ to $1.1\\,\\mu \\text{g}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\nt=3\\cdot {t}_{1/2}=3\\cdot 138.4=415.2\\,\\text{days}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-353546/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e2a81fbb-f1a8-5d80-942e-7a91bdea1408"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-353546"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-120006/selection/b0e10c3d09e2056cd2da1629f81f4dfa2bd5e0daffa0a91654598a943be2678a" :answer/id #uuid "8b936ab0-5961-5e76-b5ab-b9ea352d6f6c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "75\\,\\text{s}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-120006/selection/ed2679335ab645f02121d622747c7889b2e88588d17efe193c7b74b7a871e2fd" :answer/id #uuid "c8fbee35-a7be-5913-a607-765401f483e4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "150\\,\\text{s}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-120006/selection/b5ec3c42ca0cac93b3d3fbb91ec1eb040540e35352bb7c6616ca41ab1c16f70b" :answer/id #uuid "942d3a0b-6544-5d46-a9f9-b79d5c899554" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "100\\,\\text{s}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-120006/selection/450c08cb7b8a52b351f0e40141dd135bc38b506e383509b043a38799e5dd8953" :answer/id #uuid "b39c6a83-39e4-5586-9148-c7ad0788ccc7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "300\\,\\text{s}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-120006/selection/7f1cf73f965762e2f1c1ad8f4cc8e8e4249e1825506a25576f395c028d94120d" :answer/id #uuid "a0738644-358d-5eb4-8b63-87c22d5c64ee" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "200\\,\\text{s}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-120006/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "6f1c2e46-7bc1-5595-bd4d-114029558527" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-120006/selection/ed2679335ab645f02121d622747c7889b2e88588d17efe193c7b74b7a871e2fd" :answer-field/choices ["q-120006/selection/b0e10c3d09e2056cd2da1629f81f4dfa2bd5e0daffa0a91654598a943be2678a" "q-120006/selection/ed2679335ab645f02121d622747c7889b2e88588d17efe193c7b74b7a871e2fd" "q-120006/selection/b5ec3c42ca0cac93b3d3fbb91ec1eb040540e35352bb7c6616ca41ab1c16f70b" "q-120006/selection/450c08cb7b8a52b351f0e40141dd135bc38b506e383509b043a38799e5dd8953" "q-120006/selection/7f1cf73f965762e2f1c1ad8f4cc8e8e4249e1825506a25576f395c028d94120d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-120006" :question/id #uuid "334797b9-45e6-5677-b7ec-2655c6a7fae9" :question/math-academy-id "q-120006" :question/problem "The half-life of astatine-223 is $50\\,\\text{s}.$ How long does it take for an $400\\,\\mu \\text{g}$ sample of astatine-223 to reduce to $50\\,\\mu \\text{g}?$" :question/worked-solution "The mass $M(t)$ of a radioactive substance at time $t$ can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n{M}^{′}(t)=-kM(t),\\,M(0)={M}_{0}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nM(t)={M}_{0}{e}^{-kt}.\n$$\n\nThe half-life ${t}_{1/2}$ is the time taken for the mass of the substance to reduce by one half and is given by\n\n$$\n{t}_{1/2}=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{k}.\n$$\n\nThere are two methods that we can use to solve this problem. We describe each of them below.\n\n**Method 1**\n\nSince we know that ${t}_{1/2}=50\\,\\text{s},$ we have\n\n$$\n50=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{k}\\,⇒\\,k=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{50}\n$$\n\nAlso, since ${M}_{0}=400\\,\\mu \\text{g},$ we have the particular solution\n\n$$\nM(t)=400{e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/50}.\n$$\n\nWe wish to calculate the time $t$ such that $M(t)=50\\,\\mu \\text{g}.$ So, we set up our equation as follows:\n\n$$\n50=400{e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/50}\\,\\,\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}50 & =400{e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/50} \\\\ \\frac{50}{400} & ={e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/50} \\\\ \\frac{1}{8} & ={e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/50} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{8}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/50}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡8 & =-\\frac{t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{50} \\\\ -\\operatorname{ln}⁡8 & =-\\frac{t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{50} \\\\ t & =\\frac{50\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡8}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} \\\\ t & =150.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that it takes $150\\,\\text{s}$ for the mass of the substance to reduce to $50\\,\\mu \\text{g}.$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nWe wish to calculate the time $t$ such that $M(t)=50\\,\\mu \\text{g}.$ Notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{M(t)}{M(0)} & =\\frac{50\\,\\mu \\text{g}}{400\\,\\mu \\text{g}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8} \\\\  & ={(\\frac{1}{2})}^{3},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nor equivalently\n\n$$\nM(t)={(\\frac{1}{2})}^{3}\\cdot M(0).\n$$\n\nFrom this equation, we see that $3$ half-lives must have occurred for the mass to reduce from $400\\,\\mu \\text{g}$ to $50\\,\\mu \\text{g}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\nt=3\\cdot {t}_{1/2}=3\\cdot 50\\,\\text{s}=150\\,\\text{s}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that it takes $150\\,\\text{s}$ for the mass of the substance to reduce to $50\\,\\mu \\text{g}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-120006/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e2a81fbb-f1a8-5d80-942e-7a91bdea1408"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-120006"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-353464/field-1/09a797f0254edd22776128e046edd3557657fb445dcc367842699960b6d091f0" :answer/id #uuid "a0e4cb60-891b-57ed-83fe-ff500fbd13da" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2.6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-353464/field-1/bf404232fcde917f9ddc2adf5061d3a9eac7f7e4c6452062dfe290392ff6022c" :answer/id #uuid "94543470-7985-52d5-8828-111975cf3dbd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3.6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-353464/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "3777cd74-5ece-546f-af31-113e85e507a4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-353464/field-1/09a797f0254edd22776128e046edd3557657fb445dcc367842699960b6d091f0" :answer-field/choices ["q-353464/field-1/09a797f0254edd22776128e046edd3557657fb445dcc367842699960b6d091f0" "q-353464/field-1/bf404232fcde917f9ddc2adf5061d3a9eac7f7e4c6452062dfe290392ff6022c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-353464" :question/id #uuid "ff8bfa72-3204-5df3-86a4-a6840e17f1a6" :question/math-academy-id "q-353464" :question/problem "Iodine-131 has a half-life of $8.02$ days. A sample of iodine-131 has an initial mass of $7.2\\,\\mu \\text{g}.$ Rounded to **one decimal place**, what is the mass of the sample after $12$ days?\n\nThe mass of the sample after $12$ days is approximately\n{{field-1}}\n$\\mu \\text{g}.$" :question/worked-solution "The mass $M(t)$ of a radioactive substance at time $t$ can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n{M}^{′}(t)=-kM(t),\\,M(0)={M}_{0}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nM(t)={M}_{0}{e}^{-kt}.\n$$\n\nThe half-life ${t}_{1/2}$ is the time taken for the mass of the substance to reduce by one half and is given by\n\n$$\n{t}_{1/2}=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{k}.\n$$\n\nSince we know that ${t}_{1/2}=8.02$ days, we have\n\n$$\n8.02=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{k}\\,⇒\\,k=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{8.02}.\n$$\n\nAlso, since ${M}_{0}=7.2\\,\\mu \\text{g},$ we have the particular solution\n\n$$\nM(t)=7.2{e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/8.02}.\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\nM(12)=7.2{e}^{-12\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/8.02}≈2.6,\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the mass of the sample remaining after $12$ days is approximately $2.6\\,\\mu \\text{g}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-353464/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e2a81fbb-f1a8-5d80-942e-7a91bdea1408"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-353464"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-347510/field-1/7104e6b21d3c33ce1458066444960cb4ac3383d6d1f955bd9c663c58ca620881" :answer/id #uuid "e00d8900-6edb-5282-924b-3a8b398bd142" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "15.2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-347510/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "cb3fa8c3-30a2-54a9-a245-02d7cfa46be7" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-347510/field-1/7104e6b21d3c33ce1458066444960cb4ac3383d6d1f955bd9c663c58ca620881" :answer-field/choices ["q-347510/field-1/7104e6b21d3c33ce1458066444960cb4ac3383d6d1f955bd9c663c58ca620881"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-347510" :question/id #uuid "df5f3650-7ba5-54f7-93b4-24a31a0fac1c" :question/math-academy-id "q-347510" :question/problem "Aluminum-26 has a half-life of $0.717$ million years. A sample of aluminum-26 has an initial mass of $40\\,\\mu \\text{g}.$ Rounded to **one decimal place**, what is the mass of the sample after $1$ million years?\n\nThe mass of the sample after $1$ million years is approximately\n{{field-1}}\n$\\mu \\text{g}.$" :question/worked-solution "The mass $M(t)$ of a radioactive substance at time $t$ can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n{M}^{′}(t)=-kM(t),\\,M(0)={M}_{0}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nM(t)={M}_{0}{e}^{-kt}.\n$$\n\nThe half-life ${t}_{1/2}$ is the time taken for the mass of the substance to reduce by one half and is given by\n\n$$\n{t}_{1/2}=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{k}.\n$$\n\nSince we know that ${t}_{1/2}=0.717$ million years, we have\n\n$$\n0.717=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{k}\\,⇒\\,k=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{0.717}.\n$$\n\nAlso, since ${M}_{0}=40\\,\\mu \\text{g},$ we have the particular solution\n\n$$\nM(t)=40{e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/0.717}.\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\nM(1)=40{e}^{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/0.717}≈15.2,\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the mass of the sample remaining after $1$ million years is approximately $15.2\\,\\mu \\text{g}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-347510/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e2a81fbb-f1a8-5d80-942e-7a91bdea1408"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-347510"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-118602/selection/6514e4156c529d7305c2ffd484c350083b1fa24eec34fecbc47f56dfe28989b7" :answer/id #uuid "92f21cae-e848-50fd-b3a2-4f53911cc81a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "15\\,\\text{min}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-118602/selection/9c8349491cbf669f40f1703bd58794290146dd7c29133ab975847a212812a178" :answer/id #uuid "cedbe4fd-0ccf-5510-887f-cb8850e188ec" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "10\\,\\text{min}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-118602/selection/64c2838ecbd7a2939c2e5d441810abced34e0dfdd657a2ea53bdb38e0c8bdabd" :answer/id #uuid "bbc6c043-aaa7-55b5-ac8e-d13cd27f7493" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "13\\,\\text{min}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-118602/selection/d8e5ee21a358be9d5938f2b32594e8406c9346f6b15fc305eac71cb6e06720db" :answer/id #uuid "902616ab-c43a-54b0-95eb-1a48f18b2c7c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "8\\,\\text{min}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-118602/selection/e83b31a77d7b930ba848127643d40621db36d9be1dfe090486bcf77f21059ece" :answer/id #uuid "97c6ec5d-eae0-533a-ba43-20e800fc2420" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "7\\,\\text{min}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-118602/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "cca8d2c4-fe28-5450-b1d2-e3ddd9c51257" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-118602/selection/9c8349491cbf669f40f1703bd58794290146dd7c29133ab975847a212812a178" :answer-field/choices ["q-118602/selection/6514e4156c529d7305c2ffd484c350083b1fa24eec34fecbc47f56dfe28989b7" "q-118602/selection/9c8349491cbf669f40f1703bd58794290146dd7c29133ab975847a212812a178" "q-118602/selection/64c2838ecbd7a2939c2e5d441810abced34e0dfdd657a2ea53bdb38e0c8bdabd" "q-118602/selection/d8e5ee21a358be9d5938f2b32594e8406c9346f6b15fc305eac71cb6e06720db" "q-118602/selection/e83b31a77d7b930ba848127643d40621db36d9be1dfe090486bcf77f21059ece"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-118602" :question/id #uuid "ffb198e3-2319-570d-8d3c-85ada19388e6" :question/math-academy-id "q-118602" :question/problem "The half-life of francium-224 is $3.33\\,\\text{min}.$ Rounded to the nearest minute, how long does it take for a $160\\,\\mu \\text{g}$ sample of francium-224 to reduce to $20\\,\\mu \\text{g}?$" :question/worked-solution "The mass $M(t)$ of a radioactive substance at time $t$ can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n{M}^{′}(t)=-kM(t),\\,M(0)={M}_{0}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nM(t)={M}_{0}{e}^{-kt}.\n$$\n\nThe half-life ${t}_{1/2}$ is the time taken for the mass of the substance to reduce by one half and is given by\n\n$$\n{t}_{1/2}=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{k}.\n$$\n\nThere are two methods that we can use to solve this problem. We describe each of them below.\n\n**Method 1**\n\nSince we know that ${t}_{1/2}=3.33\\,\\text{min},$ we have\n\n$$\n3.33=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{k}\\,⇒\\,k=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{3.33}\n$$\n\nAlso, since ${M}_{0}=160\\,\\mu \\text{g},$ we have the particular solution\n\n$$\nM(t)=160{e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/3.33}.\n$$\n\nWe wish to calculate the time $t$ such that $M(t)=20\\,\\mu \\text{g}.$ So, we set up our equation as follows:\n\n$$\n20=160{e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/3.33}\\,\\,\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}20 & =160{e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/3.33} \\\\ \\frac{20}{160} & ={e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/3.33} \\\\ \\frac{1}{8} & ={e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/3.33} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{8}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/3.33}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡8 & =-\\frac{t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{3.33} \\\\ -\\operatorname{ln}⁡8 & =-\\frac{t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{3.33} \\\\ t & =\\frac{3.33\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡8}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} \\\\ t & ≈10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that it takes $10\\,\\text{min}$ for the mass of the substance to reduce to $20\\,\\mu \\text{g}.$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nWe wish to calculate the time $t$ such that $M(t)=20\\,\\mu \\text{g}.$ Notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{M(t)}{M(0)} & =\\frac{20\\,\\mu \\text{g}}{160\\,\\mu \\text{g}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8} \\\\  & ={(\\frac{1}{2})}^{3},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nor equivalently\n\n$$\nM(t)={(\\frac{1}{2})}^{3}\\cdot M(0).\n$$\n\nFrom this equation, we see that $3$ half-lives must have occurred for the mass to reduce from $160\\,\\mu \\text{g}$ to $20\\,\\mu \\text{g}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\nt=3\\cdot {t}_{1/2}=3\\cdot 3.33\\,\\text{min}≈10\\,\\text{min}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that it takes $10\\,\\text{min}$ for the mass of the substance to reduce to $20\\,\\mu \\text{g}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-118602/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e2a81fbb-f1a8-5d80-942e-7a91bdea1408"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-118602"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-118723/selection/a8e30127862ba10101de0625cb076defa75f04465865c6fe0b9d2728fd8ff6a3" :answer/id #uuid "eabd833e-06a5-59fe-862c-4a4c348e67d5" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$1.438$  billion years" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-118723/selection/a969a62a9d49b29b2435171e72cac647a196dbcd8631a4b098a2e6ba53b0fe00" :answer/id #uuid "e381ea30-abbb-5dcf-bc22-a66122897c2a" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$2.045$ billion years" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-118723/selection/000def626001451dc0f4af049a742d30aa2fc79aacd0e09c500b516e94e34d74" :answer/id #uuid "147bb96d-8a6c-57c7-b63e-6f4abe80b733" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$1.002$ billion years" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-118723/selection/fc4d9c908bdea0252de7fb2c807192b581249bd09c0f1abde0b023417df832fc" :answer/id #uuid "1f9cf74b-5043-51e7-89a4-fc61e8081f80" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$1.912$ billion years" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-118723/selection/106ca794a9c757d9999f91557d0da6f868f1c4ca9704814b7cdfd90ef9eff6bf" :answer/id #uuid "aeef3025-2cdb-50fd-a658-fa284f84ca40" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$0.721$  billion years" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-118723/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "301ad9f5-07d2-5f0a-b2c0-96e9f0f06fcd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-118723/selection/106ca794a9c757d9999f91557d0da6f868f1c4ca9704814b7cdfd90ef9eff6bf" :answer-field/choices ["q-118723/selection/a8e30127862ba10101de0625cb076defa75f04465865c6fe0b9d2728fd8ff6a3" "q-118723/selection/a969a62a9d49b29b2435171e72cac647a196dbcd8631a4b098a2e6ba53b0fe00" "q-118723/selection/000def626001451dc0f4af049a742d30aa2fc79aacd0e09c500b516e94e34d74" "q-118723/selection/fc4d9c908bdea0252de7fb2c807192b581249bd09c0f1abde0b023417df832fc" "q-118723/selection/106ca794a9c757d9999f91557d0da6f868f1c4ca9704814b7cdfd90ef9eff6bf"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-118723" :question/id #uuid "20bef9bb-a962-51c0-bda3-5c1ffebf1d72" :question/math-academy-id "q-118723" :question/problem "At a geological site, a sample of a lunar rock is found with $99%$ of its original rubidium-87 content. Given that the half-life of rubidium-87 is $49.72$ billion years, approximately how old is the sample?" :question/worked-solution "The mass $M(t)$ of a radioactive substance at time $t$ can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n{M}^{′}(t)=-kM(t),\\,M(0)={M}_{0}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nM(t)={M}_{0}{e}^{-kt}.\n$$\n\nThe half-life ${t}_{1/2}$ is the time taken for the mass of the substance to reduce by one half and is given by\n\n$$\n{t}_{1/2}=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{k}.\n$$\n\nSince we know that ${t}_{1/2}=49.72$ billion years, we have\n\n$$\n49.72=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{k}\\,⇒\\,k=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{49.72}.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nM(t)={M}_{0}{e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/49.72}.\n$$\n\nWe wish to calculate the time $t$ such that $M(t)=0.99{M}_{0}.$ So, we set up our equation as follows:\n\n$$\n0.99{M}_{0}={M}_{0}{e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/49.72}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.99{M}_{0} & ={M}_{0}{e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/49.72} \\\\ 0.99\\cancel{{M}_{0}} & =\\cancel{{M}_{0}}{e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/49.72} \\\\ 0.99 & ={e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/49.72} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.99) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/49.72}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.99) & =-\\frac{t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{49.72} \\\\ t & =-\\frac{49.72\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.99)}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} \\\\ t & ≈0.721\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the sample is approximately $0.721$ billion years old." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-118723/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a8fdf766-dca6-5dcc-ada6-c24a50c95716"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-118723"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-118727/selection/5c4151a576cf80ef83af7621320345cd5598c980c8a29bac5fbe56af775417eb" :answer/id #uuid "9e53a423-9f19-567c-b7b2-9567eac7ede8" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$2.785$  billion years" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-118727/selection/a8e30127862ba10101de0625cb076defa75f04465865c6fe0b9d2728fd8ff6a3" :answer/id #uuid "bf8af043-3884-5eb8-8c50-5490972afb46" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$1.438$  billion years" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-118727/selection/000def626001451dc0f4af049a742d30aa2fc79aacd0e09c500b516e94e34d74" :answer/id #uuid "867f5bf5-78dc-5995-be54-66f21eadbe91" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$1.002$ billion years" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-118727/selection/a969a62a9d49b29b2435171e72cac647a196dbcd8631a4b098a2e6ba53b0fe00" :answer/id #uuid "9695c18a-c76e-5f22-bead-39e99385b806" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$2.045$ billion years" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-118727/selection/8e00ce4e6107bdc43fed982e6928c0ae4e3ac6b4ed28de8976e750c948d9e4bf" :answer/id #uuid "063b93ce-b284-5fa0-9c70-be8065964dfc" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$0.721$ billion years" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-118727/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "faa3c9ae-207e-590e-9196-56c945faadaa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-118727/selection/5c4151a576cf80ef83af7621320345cd5598c980c8a29bac5fbe56af775417eb" :answer-field/choices ["q-118727/selection/5c4151a576cf80ef83af7621320345cd5598c980c8a29bac5fbe56af775417eb" "q-118727/selection/a8e30127862ba10101de0625cb076defa75f04465865c6fe0b9d2728fd8ff6a3" "q-118727/selection/000def626001451dc0f4af049a742d30aa2fc79aacd0e09c500b516e94e34d74" "q-118727/selection/a969a62a9d49b29b2435171e72cac647a196dbcd8631a4b098a2e6ba53b0fe00" "q-118727/selection/8e00ce4e6107bdc43fed982e6928c0ae4e3ac6b4ed28de8976e750c948d9e4bf"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-118727" :question/id #uuid "5490c908-aa19-562a-8106-03465a2136f6" :question/math-academy-id "q-118727" :question/problem "At a geological site, an object is found with $95%$ of its original lutetium-176 content. Given that the half-life of lutetium-176 is $37.64$ billion years, approximately how old is the object?" :question/worked-solution "The mass $M(t)$ of a radioactive substance at time $t$ can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n{M}^{′}(t)=-kM(t),\\,M(0)={M}_{0}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nM(t)={M}_{0}{e}^{-kt}.\n$$\n\nThe half-life ${t}_{1/2}$ is the time taken for the mass of the substance to reduce by one half and is given by\n\n$$\n{t}_{1/2}=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{k}.\n$$\n\nSince we know that ${t}_{1/2}=37.64$ billion years, we have\n\n$$\n37.64=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{k}\\,⇒\\,k=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{37.64}.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nM(t)={M}_{0}{e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/37.64}.\n$$\n\nWe wish to calculate the time $t$ such that $M(t)=0.95{M}_{0}.$ So, we set up our equation as follows:\n\n$$\n0.95{M}_{0}={M}_{0}{e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/37.64}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.95{M}_{0} & ={M}_{0}{e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/37.64} \\\\ 0.95\\cancel{{M}_{0}} & =\\cancel{{M}_{0}}{e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/37.64} \\\\ 0.95 & ={e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/37.64} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.95) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/37.64}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.95) & =-\\frac{t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{37.64} \\\\ t & =-\\frac{37.64\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.95)}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} \\\\ t & ≈2.785\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the object is approximately $2.785$ billion years old." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-118727/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a8fdf766-dca6-5dcc-ada6-c24a50c95716"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-118727"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-15221/selection/1b5263ee761a8a0f73a920e25c917610b797346bb1190b03f3de53cd2cacad35" :answer/id #uuid "be9d2e0b-aff4-5f10-bfee-1c2be30cfc13" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$9\\,919$ years" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-15221/selection/0caeb1e1366a00c1538770f8de31aecc2d003f83554cf7ca2182f39c8d1b193d" :answer/id #uuid "4ec8b60d-b4e2-5eee-8063-0a7b869a26fc" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$9\\,827$ years" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-15221/selection/73965b78cea87f43c629aa17e9c2a5d738c8289db53dd0c6301f6adea4ae9864" :answer/id #uuid "e7ddc236-8946-5cd8-95e4-f369dfdb9a1f" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$9\\,539$ years" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-15221/selection/ca43110c317cae578a68e0782ab2281e300285232f37a3fc46c26e7df42cb8c3" :answer/id #uuid "36c52d62-2791-5fcf-aaae-0daa302a4e53" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$9\\,632$ years" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-15221/selection/bcd75d6fe0f4a40237f7b91d295e43b7954158568dd278f2c2a79e3e93c85f2f" :answer/id #uuid "a3fc3dd7-bd0e-5318-abdd-ac5bc83e04ec" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$9\\,953$ years" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-15221/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "54300fc0-f7c0-5a50-bdaa-05eef1861f3a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-15221/selection/bcd75d6fe0f4a40237f7b91d295e43b7954158568dd278f2c2a79e3e93c85f2f" :answer-field/choices ["q-15221/selection/1b5263ee761a8a0f73a920e25c917610b797346bb1190b03f3de53cd2cacad35" "q-15221/selection/0caeb1e1366a00c1538770f8de31aecc2d003f83554cf7ca2182f39c8d1b193d" "q-15221/selection/73965b78cea87f43c629aa17e9c2a5d738c8289db53dd0c6301f6adea4ae9864" "q-15221/selection/ca43110c317cae578a68e0782ab2281e300285232f37a3fc46c26e7df42cb8c3" "q-15221/selection/bcd75d6fe0f4a40237f7b91d295e43b7954158568dd278f2c2a79e3e93c85f2f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-15221" :question/id #uuid "0ddd11ae-897a-5ef2-9c54-19be7cf4ebb9" :question/math-academy-id "q-15221" :question/problem "At an archaeological site, an object is found with $30%$ of its original carbon-14 content. Given that the half-life of carbon-14 is $5\\,730$ years, approximately how old is the object?" :question/worked-solution "The mass $M(t)$ of a radioactive substance at time $t$ can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n{M}^{′}(t)=-kM(t),\\,M(0)={M}_{0}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nM(t)={M}_{0}{e}^{-kt}.\n$$\n\nThe half-life ${t}_{1/2}$ is the time taken for the mass of the substance to reduce by one half and is given by\n\n$$\n{t}_{1/2}=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{k}.\n$$\n\nSince we know that ${t}_{1/2}=5\\,730$ years, we have\n\n$$\n5\\,730=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{k}\\,⇒\\,k=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{5\\,730}.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nM(t)={M}_{0}{e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/5\\,730}.\n$$\n\nWe wish to calculate the time $t$ such that $M(t)=0.3{M}_{0}.$ So, we set up our equation as follows:\n\n$$\n0.3{M}_{0}={M}_{0}{e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/5\\,730}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.3{M}_{0} & ={M}_{0}{e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/5\\,730} \\\\ 0.3\\cancel{{M}_{0}} & =\\cancel{{M}_{0}}{e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/5\\,730} \\\\ 0.3 & ={e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/5\\,730} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.3) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/5\\,730}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.3) & =-\\frac{t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{5\\,730} \\\\ t & =-\\frac{5\\,730\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.3)}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} \\\\ t & ≈9\\,953\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the object is approximately $9\\,953$ years old." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-15221/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a8fdf766-dca6-5dcc-ada6-c24a50c95716"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-15221"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-353610/field-1/a4701b6ace74588b250e01600a16ebac073f8b70a48851f31fb55f5bc77bc444" :answer/id #uuid "3236557b-bbb2-58f5-934a-52aef9b39d6b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5.2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-353610/field-1/09a797f0254edd22776128e046edd3557657fb445dcc367842699960b6d091f0" :answer/id #uuid "df376629-0fcb-598a-a5ac-a711821ad3e4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2.6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-353610/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "5e732dbe-b863-5dff-b701-0fe0d774c8de" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-353610/field-1/a4701b6ace74588b250e01600a16ebac073f8b70a48851f31fb55f5bc77bc444" :answer-field/choices ["q-353610/field-1/a4701b6ace74588b250e01600a16ebac073f8b70a48851f31fb55f5bc77bc444" "q-353610/field-1/09a797f0254edd22776128e046edd3557657fb445dcc367842699960b6d091f0"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-353610" :question/id #uuid "db8660ee-2d08-5e8e-b776-b18ced9354e2" :question/math-academy-id "q-353610" :question/problem "In a medical imaging facility, a tracer is found with $25%$ of its original sodium-22 content. Given that the half-life of sodium-22 is $2.6$ years, how old is the tracer? Round your answer to **one decimal place**.\n\nThe tracer is approximately\n{{field-1}}\nyears old." :question/worked-solution "The mass $M(t)$ of a radioactive substance at time $t$ can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n{M}^{′}(t)=-kM(t),\\,M(0)={M}_{0}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nM(t)={M}_{0}{e}^{-kt}.\n$$\n\nThe half-life ${t}_{1/2}$ is the time taken for the mass of the substance to reduce by one half and is given by\n\n$$\n{t}_{1/2}=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{k}.\n$$\n\nSince we know that ${t}_{1/2}=2.6$ years, we have\n\n$$\n2.6=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{k}\\,⇒\\,k=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{2.6}.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nM(t)={M}_{0}{e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/2.6}.\n$$\n\nWe wish to calculate the time $t$ such that $M(t)=0.25{M}_{0}.$ So, we set up our equation as follows:\n\n$$\n0.25{M}_{0}={M}_{0}{e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/2.6}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.25{M}_{0} & ={M}_{0}{e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/2.6} \\\\ 0.25\\cancel{{M}_{0}} & =\\cancel{{M}_{0}}{e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/2.6} \\\\ 0.25 & ={e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/2.6} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.25) & =ln\\,({e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/2.6}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.25) & =-\\frac{t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{2.6} \\\\ t & =-\\frac{2.6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.25)}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} \\\\ t & ≈5.2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the tracer is approximately $5.2$ years old." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-353610/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a8fdf766-dca6-5dcc-ada6-c24a50c95716"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-353610"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-355652/field-1/9db8bb3a8fca0a53f1a66296f215433b5fba29bca2325a9035c810088be55a44" :answer/id #uuid "335a42b6-f1d5-51ab-9549-e62e8bec76ba" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2.9" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-355652/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "f941087b-ba55-5e90-bb4f-b5b4bf8de283" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-355652/field-1/9db8bb3a8fca0a53f1a66296f215433b5fba29bca2325a9035c810088be55a44" :answer-field/choices ["q-355652/field-1/9db8bb3a8fca0a53f1a66296f215433b5fba29bca2325a9035c810088be55a44"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-355652" :question/id #uuid "401be605-ed19-578b-aa65-0903ca85fa5c" :question/math-academy-id "q-355652" :question/problem "At a geologic study site, a sample is found with $42%$ of its original cesium-135 content. Given that the half-life of cesium-135 is $2.3$ million years, how old is the sample? Round your answer to **one decimal place**.\n\nThe sample is approximately\n{{field-1}}\nmillion years old." :question/worked-solution "The mass $M(t)$ of a radioactive substance at time $t$ can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n{M}^{′}(t)=-kM(t),\\,M(0)={M}_{0}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nM(t)={M}_{0}{e}^{-kt}.\n$$\n\nThe half-life ${t}_{1/2}$ is the time taken for the mass of the substance to reduce by one half and is given by\n\n$$\n{t}_{1/2}=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{k}.\n$$\n\nSince we know that ${t}_{1/2}=2.3$ million years, we have\n\n$$\n2.3=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{k}\\,⇒\\,k=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{2.3}.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nM(t)={M}_{0}{e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/2.3}.\n$$\n\nWe wish to calculate the time $t$ such that $M(t)=0.42{M}_{0}.$ So, we set up our equation as follows:\n\n$$\n0.42{M}_{0}={M}_{0}{e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/2.3}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.42{M}_{0} & ={M}_{0}{e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/2.3} \\\\ 0.42\\cancel{{M}_{0}} & =\\cancel{{M}_{0}}{e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/2.3} \\\\ 0.42 & ={e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/2.3} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.42) & =ln\\,({e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/2.3}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.42) & =-\\frac{t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{2.3} \\\\ t & =-\\frac{2.3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.42)}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} \\\\ t & ≈2.9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the sample is approximately $2.9$ million years old." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-355652/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a8fdf766-dca6-5dcc-ada6-c24a50c95716"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-355652"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-9374"] :question/problem "The mass $M(t),$ in grams, of a sample of iron-60 is given by\n\n$$\nM(t) = 100 e^{-kt},\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ is the time, in millions of years. The half-life of iron-60 is $2.6$ million years. Rounded to three significant figures, what is the value of $k?$" :question/worked-solution "The mass $M(t)$ of a radioactive substance at time $t$ can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\nM'(t) = -kM(t), \\qquad M(0) = M_0.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nM(t) = M_0e^{-kt}.\n$$\n\nThe half-life $t_{1/2}$ is the time taken for the mass of the substance to reduce by one half and is given by\n\n$$\nt_{1/2} = \\dfrac{\\ln 2}{k}.\n$$\n\nSince we know that $t_{1/2} = 2.6$ million years, we have\n\n$$\n2.6 = \\dfrac{\\ln 2}{k}\\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad k = \\dfrac{\\ln 2}{2.6} \\approx 0.267.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-6675"] :question/problem "The half-life of bismuth-210 is $5.012$ days. How long does it take for a $1\\,280\\,\\textrm g$ sample of bismuth-210 to reduce to $5\\,\\textrm g?$" :question/worked-solution "The mass $M(t)$ of a radioactive substance at time $t$ can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\nM'(t) = -kM(t), \\qquad M(0) = M_0.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nM(t) = M_0e^{-kt}.\n$$\n\nThe half-life $t_{1/2}$ is the time taken for the mass of the substance to reduce by one half and is given by\n\n$$\nt_{1/2} = \\dfrac{\\ln 2}{k}.\n$$\n\nThere are two methods that we can use to solve this problem. We describe each of them below.\n\n**Method 1**\n\nSince we know that $t_{1/2} = 5.012$ days we have\n\n$$\n5.012 = \\dfrac{\\ln 2}{k}\\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad k = \\dfrac{\\ln 2}{5.012}\n$$\n\nAlso, since $M_0 = 1\\,280\\,\\textrm g,$ we have the particular solution\n\n$$\nM(t) = 1\\,280 e^{-t\\ln 2/5.012}.\n$$\n\nWe wish to calculate the time $t$ such that $M(t) = 5\\,\\textrm g.$ So, we set up our equation as follows:\n\n$$\n5 = 1\\,280 e^{-t\\ln 2/5.012}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5 & =1\\,280{e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/5.012} \\\\ \\frac{5}{1\\,280} & ={e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/5.012} \\\\ \\frac{1}{256} & ={e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/5.012} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{256}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/5.012}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡256 & =-\\frac{t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{5.012} \\\\ -\\operatorname{ln}⁡256 & =-\\frac{t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{5.012} \\\\ t & =\\frac{5.012\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡256}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} \\\\ t & ≈40.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that it takes approximately $40$ days for the mass of the substance to reduce to $5\\,\\textrm g.$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nWe wish to calculate the time $t$ such that $M(t) = 5\\,\\textrm g.$ Notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{M(t)}{M(0)} & =\\frac{5\\,\\text{g}}{1\\,280\\,\\text{g}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{256} \\\\  & ={(\\frac{1}{2})}^{8},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nor equivalently\n\n$$\nM(t) =  \\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)^8\\cdot M(0).\n$$\n\nFrom this equation, we see that $8$ half-lives must have occurred for the mass to reduce from $1\\,280\\,\\textrm g$ to $5\\,\\textrm g.$ Hence,\n\n$$\nt = 8\\cdot t_{1/2} =8 \\cdot 5.012 \\approx 40.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that it takes approximately $40$ days for the mass of the substance to reduce to $5\\,\\textrm g.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-9373"] :question/worked-solution "The mass $M(t)$ of a radioactive substance at time $t$ can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\nM'(t) = -kM(t), \\qquad M(0) = M_0.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nM(t) = M_0e^{-kt}.\n$$\n\nThe half-life $t_{1/2}$ is the time taken for the mass of the substance to reduce by one half and is given by\n\n$$\nt_{1/2} = \\dfrac{\\ln 2}{k}.\n$$\n\nSince we know that $t_{1/2} = 301$ thousand years, we have\n\n$$\n301 = \\dfrac{\\ln 2}{k}\\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad k = \\dfrac{\\ln 2}{301}.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nM(t) = M_0 e^{-t\\ln 2/301}.\n$$\n\nWe wish to calculate the time $t$ such that $M(t) = 0.65 M_0.$ So, we set up our equation as follows:\n\n$$\n0.65M_0 = M_0 e^{-t\\ln 2/301}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.65{M}_{0} & ={M}_{0}{e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/301} \\\\ 0.65\\cancel{{M}_{0}} & =\\cancel{{M}_{0}}{e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/301} \\\\ 0.65 & ={e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/301} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.65) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/301}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.65) & =-\\frac{t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{301} \\\\ t & =-\\frac{301\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.65)}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} \\\\ t & ≈187.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the object is approximately $187$ thousand years old." :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
